1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,023
What does the team do in the first ten
minutes? Decide on your own first move, and

2
00:00:09,063 --> 00:00:09,913
say it out loud.

3
00:00:14,753 --> 00:00:19,857
In most teams, someone opens the repository.
They look for the file, discuss whether

4
00:00:19,897 --> 00:00:24,696
to delete it or to rewrite history, argue
about force pushes, and ask who has a

5
00:00:24,736 --> 00:00:27,881
clone. An hour later the
history is being rewritten.

6
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:29,105
The key still works.

7
00:00:29,445 --> 00:00:32,621
The cases in this video
show what that order costs.

8
00:00:32,661 --> 00:00:35,836
A key valid about two months
after the first alert.

9
00:00:35,876 --> 00:00:39,745
Cloud keys valid 48 hours after
the repository was taken down.

10
00:00:39,785 --> 00:00:44,347
An organization breached a second time
through tokens it had not rotated.

11
00:00:44,687 --> 00:00:48,444
There are six steps, and their
order matters more than the tools.

12
00:00:48,484 --> 00:00:51,774
The first step is the one that
teams under pressure skip.

13
00:00:51,814 --> 00:00:56,272
Hold on to your own first move. You'll
check it against step one in a moment.

14
00:00:58,112 --> 00:01:01,628
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:01,668 --> 00:01:05,008
This is the video that the last
three have been preparing.

16
00:01:05,048 --> 00:01:09,554
You know what a stolen credential can
reach. You know that nothing you do to a

17
00:01:09,594 --> 00:01:14,391
repository un-publishes a pushed commit.
You know how to measure where a secret is.

18
00:01:14,431 --> 00:01:16,839
Now you put those in order, as a response.

19
00:01:17,179 --> 00:01:22,381
Three words first. A secret here is a
credential, such as an API key: a string that

20
00:01:22,421 --> 00:01:27,245
proves to a service who is asking. The
issuer is the service that created it.

21
00:01:27,285 --> 00:01:32,298
To revoke a key is to cancel it at the
issuer, and to rotate it is to replace it

22
00:01:32,338 --> 00:01:33,245
with a new one.

23
00:01:33,585 --> 00:01:40,342
Two sections. Section 21B.14: responding
to a leaked secret in six steps: contain,

24
00:01:40,382 --> 00:01:47,305
assess, eradicate, recover, communicate,
prevent. Section 21B.15: nine case studies,

25
00:01:47,345 --> 00:01:50,621
each as exposure, root cause and lesson.

26
00:01:50,961 --> 00:01:55,661
One note on the names. The textbook says
the names of the phases follow common

27
00:01:55,701 --> 00:02:00,584
incident-response practice. The Phase 0
report did not check them against a cited

28
00:02:00,624 --> 00:02:04,474
standard, and legal notification
duties are outside this course.

29
00:02:04,814 --> 00:02:09,863
The demonstration replays the first three
steps of the tabletop lab, a rehearsal of

30
00:02:09,903 --> 00:02:14,031
an incident with nothing real at
stake. It stops before eradication.

31
00:02:14,071 --> 00:02:18,875
A history rewrite is an operation with
its own dangers, and the next two videos

32
00:02:18,915 --> 00:02:19,426
teach it.

33
00:02:21,266 --> 00:02:22,845
Here's what you'll walk away with.

34
00:02:23,185 --> 00:02:24,597
After this video you can:

35
00:02:24,937 --> 00:02:30,422
recite the six steps in order: contain,
assess, eradicate, recover, communicate,

36
00:02:30,462 --> 00:02:30,975
prevent;

37
00:02:31,315 --> 00:02:35,037
explain why revocation comes first
and why it may be sufficient;

38
00:02:35,377 --> 00:02:39,419
produce the five facts of an assessment
and say which of them Git can answer;

39
00:02:39,759 --> 00:02:42,262
decide whether a history
rewrite is warranted;

40
00:02:42,602 --> 00:02:48,128
retell two case studies as exposure, root
cause and lesson, quoting figures as the

41
00:02:48,168 --> 00:02:49,757
cited sources give them.

42
00:02:51,597 --> 00:02:55,323
Step 1: contain. Revoke or
rotate the credential first.

43
00:02:55,363 --> 00:03:00,731
Was that your first move? The sources,
GitHub and OWASP, add: that alone may be

44
00:03:00,771 --> 00:03:04,497
sufficient, and a history
rewrite may not be warranted.

45
00:03:04,837 --> 00:03:08,956
After it come assess, eradicate,
recover, communicate and prevent.

46
00:03:09,296 --> 00:03:14,492
The evidence of what goes wrong otherwise.
An xAI key valid about two months after

47
00:03:14,532 --> 00:03:19,599
the first alert. A contractor's AWS keys
valid 48 hours after the repository was

48
00:03:19,639 --> 00:03:24,835
taken down. And the Internet Archive,
breached a second time through tokens it had

49
00:03:24,875 --> 00:03:25,601
not rotated.

50
00:03:25,941 --> 00:03:30,704
Step 2: assess. Identify the secret,
its owner, and what it can reach.

51
00:03:30,744 --> 00:03:35,851
Check validity status and exposure
labels. Review GitHub audit logs and the

52
00:03:35,891 --> 00:03:41,478
provider's logs for use. And include forks,
deleted forks and force-pushed commits

53
00:03:41,518 --> 00:03:42,095
in scope.

54
00:03:42,435 --> 00:03:46,864
What goes wrong otherwise: an affected
company declined to say whether logs showed

55
00:03:46,904 --> 00:03:51,332
third-party use of an exposed token. When
you can't answer "was it used?", that is

56
00:03:51,372 --> 00:03:52,858
the answer the public hears.

57
00:03:53,198 --> 00:03:56,672
Step 3: eradicate. Remove the
secret from current code.

58
00:03:56,712 --> 00:04:01,401
Rewrite history, meaning replace the
old commits with new ones, only where

59
00:04:01,441 --> 00:04:06,321
warranted, with git-filter-repo 2.47 or
later, then force-push, then a GitHub

60
00:04:06,361 --> 00:04:11,689
Support request. And note what the documentation
says about Support: it assists only

61
00:04:11,729 --> 00:04:14,181
where rotation can't mitigate the risk.

62
00:04:14,521 --> 00:04:19,237
The scope warning attached to this step:
after a supply-chain compromise the scope

63
00:04:19,277 --> 00:04:22,484
is every credential reachable
from the affected runtime.

64
00:04:22,824 --> 00:04:27,026
Step 4: recover. Update dependent
services with the new credential.

65
00:04:27,066 --> 00:04:32,026
If history was rewritten, have collaborators
re-clone, and re-enable force-push

66
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:35,381
protection. Close the alert
as revoked, and document.

67
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:39,584
What goes wrong otherwise: partial
rotation produced a second breach.

68
00:04:39,924 --> 00:04:45,627
Step 5: communicate. Track internally.
Tell collaborators exactly what to do with

69
00:04:45,667 --> 00:04:50,661
their clones. Keep a reachable
disclosure contact, such as SECURITY.md.

70
00:04:51,001 --> 00:04:56,344
What goes wrong otherwise: a researcher
couldn't find a way to report a contractor's

71
00:04:56,384 --> 00:05:01,214
leak and went to the press. And concealment
turned Uber's 2016 breach into a

72
00:05:01,254 --> 00:05:02,239
regulatory case.

73
00:05:02,579 --> 00:05:06,950
Step 6: prevent. Push protection.
Pre-commit and CI scanning.

74
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:11,143
Secrets out of code. Short-lived
credentials through OIDC.

75
00:05:11,183 --> 00:05:17,217
Least privilege. Staging named files and
reviewing git diff --cached, instead of git

76
00:05:17,257 --> 00:05:20,181
add . with a dot. And scheduled rotation.

77
00:05:20,521 --> 00:05:24,939
The evidence: hardcoded long-lived keys
recur in every case of the next section.

78
00:05:25,279 --> 00:05:29,474
Why containment comes first. This is the
argument you must be able to make to

79
00:05:29,514 --> 00:05:32,059
someone who wants to start
with the repository.

80
00:05:32,399 --> 00:05:37,266
The repository isn't where the damage
happens. The damage happens at the issuer,

81
00:05:37,306 --> 00:05:42,174
where the key is accepted. Until revocation
the key works for whoever copied it.

82
00:05:42,214 --> 00:05:47,143
And revocation is the only step that is
complete: it works against clones, forks,

83
00:05:47,183 --> 00:05:48,921
caches and screenshots alike.

84
00:05:49,261 --> 00:05:52,603
GitHub's own guidance says
the same: rotate immediately.

85
00:05:52,643 --> 00:05:57,494
Removing the secret from history is
time-consuming and often unnecessary once the

86
00:05:57,534 --> 00:05:58,822
credential is revoked.

87
00:05:59,162 --> 00:06:02,662
What assessment has to produce.
Five facts, written down.

88
00:06:03,002 --> 00:06:05,301
One: which secret, and what it can reach.

89
00:06:05,641 --> 00:06:09,747
Two: the first commit that contains it,
and when that commit was first pushed.

90
00:06:10,087 --> 00:06:11,905
Three: which refs contain it.

91
00:06:12,245 --> 00:06:17,496
Four: who could read it: repository
visibility, forks, collaborators, CI logs.

92
00:06:17,836 --> 00:06:20,710
Five: whether it was used,
from the provider's logs.

93
00:06:21,050 --> 00:06:25,553
And the sentence that divides the work:
Git answers the second and the third.

94
00:06:25,593 --> 00:06:27,501
Only the issuer answers the last.

95
00:06:27,841 --> 00:06:32,502
When a history rewrite is warranted.
Rewrite when the data stays harmful after

96
00:06:32,542 --> 00:06:37,566
rotation, or can't be rotated. The textbook's
list: personal data, customer records,

97
00:06:37,606 --> 00:06:42,630
proprietary model weights, a private key
whose public half is pinned in devices, and

98
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:44,920
a secret whose revocation takes weeks.

99
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:50,533
Quick quiz. An ordinary API key leaked.
It was revoked within the hour.

100
00:06:50,573 --> 00:06:54,424
Do you rewrite history? A,
yes, always, to be clean.

101
00:06:54,464 --> 00:06:56,668
B, not by reflex. Your answer?

102
00:07:01,508 --> 00:07:06,106
B. Don't rewrite by reflex for an API
key that was revoked within the hour.

103
00:07:06,146 --> 00:07:11,238
The rewrite costs every collaborator their
clone, invalidates every recorded commit

104
00:07:11,278 --> 00:07:15,319
ID, strips signatures, and can't
recall copies that already exist.

105
00:07:15,659 --> 00:07:17,894
Either way: record the
decision and its reason.

106
00:07:18,234 --> 00:07:19,287
The case studies.

107
00:07:19,627 --> 00:07:23,700
The textbook says the root-cause column
repeats one pattern: a long-lived,

108
00:07:23,740 --> 00:07:27,201
over-scoped credential written
where it should never have been.

109
00:07:27,241 --> 00:07:27,979
Listen for it.

110
00:07:28,319 --> 00:07:33,060
Uber, 2016. Exposed: a cloud access
key in a private GitHub repository.

111
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:37,099
Intruders downloaded tens of
millions of records in a month.

112
00:07:37,139 --> 00:07:42,553
Root cause: a plaintext key in source code,
reused passwords, and no multi-factor

113
00:07:42,593 --> 00:07:48,074
requirement on GitHub accounts. Lesson: a
private repository is only as private as

114
00:07:48,114 --> 00:07:49,892
its weakest member account.

115
00:07:50,232 --> 00:07:56,072
Toyota, 2022. Exposed: a data-server
access key, public from December 2017 to

116
00:07:56,112 --> 00:08:00,578
September 2022, with 296,019
customers potentially exposed.

117
00:08:00,618 --> 00:08:06,154
Root cause: a subcontractor published
internal code with a hardcoded key.

118
00:08:06,194 --> 00:08:11,805
Lesson: keys that never expire turn
one mistake into a five-year exposure.

119
00:08:12,145 --> 00:08:17,354
Samsung, 2022. Exposed: 6,695
secrets inside stolen source code.

120
00:08:17,394 --> 00:08:22,520
Root cause: thousands of credentials
hardcoded in private code.

121
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:25,554
Lesson: assume source code will leak.

122
00:08:25,894 --> 00:08:31,363
Microsoft AI research, 2020 to 2023.
Exposed: a storage URL in a public model

123
00:08:31,403 --> 00:08:36,514
repository that granted full control
of an account holding 38 terabytes.

124
00:08:36,554 --> 00:08:42,309
Root cause: an over-scoped, long-lived
sharing token committed to the repository.

125
00:08:42,349 --> 00:08:47,747
Lesson: a data-sharing URL is a credential.
Share weights through read-only,

126
00:08:47,787 --> 00:08:49,178
expiring mechanisms.

127
00:08:49,518 --> 00:08:55,817
Hugging Face tokens, 2023. Exposed:
1,681 valid tokens giving access to 723

128
00:08:55,857 --> 00:09:02,496
organizations, 655 with write permission.
Root cause: tokens committed to code.

129
00:09:02,536 --> 00:09:07,652
Lesson: a model-hub write token
is a supply-chain credential.

130
00:09:07,992 --> 00:09:13,626
Mercedes-Benz, 2024. Exposed: an employee
token, public for about four months, that

131
00:09:13,666 --> 00:09:18,207
gave unrestricted access to the
company's GitHub Enterprise Server.

132
00:09:18,247 --> 00:09:22,377
Root cause: a broad, long-lived
token in a public repository.

133
00:09:22,417 --> 00:09:26,411
Lesson: one over-scoped token
equals the whole code estate.

134
00:09:26,751 --> 00:09:32,691
The New York Times, 2024. Exposed: a 273
gigabyte archive of repositories, leaked

135
00:09:32,731 --> 00:09:38,893
five months after a GitHub credential was
exposed. Root cause: an exposed token with

136
00:09:38,933 --> 00:09:44,874
organization-wide read access. Lesson:
least-privilege, expiring tokens bound the

137
00:09:44,914 --> 00:09:45,390
damage.

138
00:09:45,730 --> 00:09:51,585
xAI, 2025. Exposed: an LLM API key with
access to at least 60 private and fine-tuned

139
00:09:51,625 --> 00:09:57,268
models, valid two months after the first
alert. Root cause: the key was hardcoded

140
00:09:57,308 --> 00:10:01,268
and pushed, and the alert was
sent only to an individual.

141
00:10:01,308 --> 00:10:05,197
Lesson: an alert in one inbox
isn't an incident process.

142
00:10:05,537 --> 00:10:11,074
A contractor of CISA, 2025 to 2026. Exposed:
administrative cloud credentials and

143
00:10:11,114 --> 00:10:15,068
plaintext passwords in a public
repository for six months.

144
00:10:15,108 --> 00:10:20,300
Root cause: work material copied to a
personal public repository with secret

145
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:26,015
detection deliberately disabled. Lesson:
controls an individual can switch off need

146
00:10:26,055 --> 00:10:29,113
organizational backstops
and a leak playbook.

147
00:10:29,453 --> 00:10:35,166
The flags. The report marks these details
as partially confirmed, so you quote them

148
00:10:35,206 --> 00:10:40,364
as the cited articles do. The day-level
timeline of the Mercedes-Benz case.

149
00:10:40,404 --> 00:10:46,186
The size of the New York Times archive, 273
gigabytes in the cited article and often

150
00:10:46,226 --> 00:10:51,453
rounded to 270. The Internet Archive
user count. Samsung's own confirmation.

151
00:10:51,493 --> 00:10:57,275
And Toyota's original notice. And one negative
finding: the report found no verified

152
00:10:57,315 --> 00:11:02,265
2026 incident caused specifically by
a key in a public Jupyter notebook.

153
00:11:02,305 --> 00:11:07,325
Notebooks are documented as a leak
mechanism, not through a named breach.

154
00:11:07,665 --> 00:11:11,252
For an LLM engineer, the textbook
draws three conclusions.

155
00:11:11,292 --> 00:11:16,443
An LLM API key reaches more than a bill:
the xAI key reached private and fine-tuned

156
00:11:16,483 --> 00:11:21,322
models. A link that shares weights or
data is a credential, with a scope and a

157
00:11:21,362 --> 00:11:26,450
lifetime. And a model-hub token with write
permission lets its holder replace what

158
00:11:26,490 --> 00:11:27,701
your users download.

159
00:11:29,541 --> 00:11:34,604
Section 21B.14 gives no analogy of its
own, so here is one for this video.

160
00:11:34,644 --> 00:11:40,189
Imagine you lost the key to your house,
with a tag on it that gives your address.

161
00:11:40,529 --> 00:11:44,933
You can spend the morning looking for the
key: the car, the office, the street.

162
00:11:44,973 --> 00:11:49,377
You may even find it. You still don't
know who saw it or copied it while it was

163
00:11:49,417 --> 00:11:49,659
gone.

164
00:11:49,999 --> 00:11:54,780
Or you can change the lock. That takes one
call, and from that moment every copy of

165
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:57,278
the old key, wherever
it is, opens nothing.

166
00:11:57,618 --> 00:12:02,180
Change the lock first. Then look for the
key, to learn what was at risk and for how

167
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:02,457
long.

168
00:12:02,797 --> 00:12:06,230
Where the picture falls short:
a house key opens one door.

169
00:12:06,270 --> 00:12:11,079
A credential may open many, and it may
have opened them already without leaving a

170
00:12:11,119 --> 00:12:16,047
trace you can see from your side. That's
why step two includes the provider's logs,

171
00:12:16,087 --> 00:12:19,220
and why the fifth fact can
only come from the issuer.

172
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:24,296
And the picture explains the rewrite decision.
Changing the lock does nothing for a

173
00:12:24,336 --> 00:12:29,015
diary that was lying in the open. Data that
is harmful in itself, such as personal

174
00:12:29,055 --> 00:12:33,676
data or model weights, can't be revoked.
For that, and for that only, the work on

175
00:12:33,716 --> 00:12:35,517
the repository is the main work.

176
00:12:37,357 --> 00:12:42,357
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
a line with six stations and write the

177
00:12:42,397 --> 00:12:46,525
six steps on it, in order. Under
station two, write the five facts.

178
00:12:46,565 --> 00:12:50,196
Then mark the facts that Git
can answer. Say them out loud.

179
00:12:55,036 --> 00:13:00,486
Check your track. Contain, assess,
eradicate, recover, communicate, prevent.

180
00:13:00,526 --> 00:13:05,831
The key stops working at station one,
against every clone, fork, cache and

181
00:13:05,871 --> 00:13:06,626
screenshot.

182
00:13:06,966 --> 00:13:11,566
And Git answers two of the five facts: the
first commit, and which refs contain it.

183
00:13:11,606 --> 00:13:16,151
One caution about the first: when that
commit was first pushed is a question about

184
00:13:16,191 --> 00:13:16,766
the server.

185
00:13:18,606 --> 00:13:20,239
Every secret here is a dummy.

186
00:13:20,579 --> 00:13:22,424
Step 0: confirm.

187
00:13:22,764 --> 00:13:24,658
All three read. 🟢 SAFE.

188
00:13:24,998 --> 00:13:29,857
The report is true: the key is in .env
on the current tip, it entered in commit

189
00:13:29,897 --> 00:13:34,446
0805fd8, and the branch is in sync with
the server, so it has been pushed.

190
00:13:34,486 --> 00:13:38,974
That took three commands. Don't let
confirmation turn into investigation.

191
00:13:39,014 --> 00:13:40,400
You know enough to act.

192
00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:42,205
Step 1: contain.

193
00:13:42,545 --> 00:13:47,127
Before the output: at this moment nothing
in the repository has been touched.

194
00:13:47,167 --> 00:13:50,788
The file is still tracked. The
commit is still on the server.

195
00:13:50,828 --> 00:13:54,210
Is the incident better or worse
than it was a minute ago?

196
00:13:54,250 --> 00:13:55,170
Say it out loud.

197
00:14:00,010 --> 00:14:03,838
"ACTIVE", then "revoked", then a
new key issued, then "REVOKED".

198
00:14:03,878 --> 00:14:07,283
The old key now opens nothing,
for anyone, from any copy.

199
00:14:07,323 --> 00:14:12,057
The repository is exactly as it was, and
the incident is fundamentally smaller.

200
00:14:12,097 --> 00:14:17,013
Everything after this line is assessment
and cleanup, done without a clock running

201
00:14:17,053 --> 00:14:17,738
against you.

202
00:14:18,078 --> 00:14:22,672
A real revocation isn't a Git command.
It happens in the provider's console.

203
00:14:22,712 --> 00:14:27,732
It also changes what every service that
uses the key can do, which is why step four

204
00:14:27,772 --> 00:14:30,842
exists: dependent services
need the new credential.

205
00:14:31,182 --> 00:14:32,691
Step 2: assess.

206
00:14:33,031 --> 00:14:38,083
All read. Seven commands. Match each to
one of the five facts before you see the

207
00:14:38,123 --> 00:14:42,157
output. Which facts get no command
at all? Make your prediction.

208
00:14:46,997 --> 00:14:48,165
Read it as a report.

209
00:14:48,505 --> 00:14:54,306
The first commit: 0805fd8, by the lab user,
committed on the seventh of September

210
00:14:54,346 --> 00:14:59,931
2026 at ten past ten, "Add staging
settings". That's the first half of fact b.

211
00:15:00,271 --> 00:15:05,799
Which refs: main, the remote-tracking main,
a remote-tracking feature branch, and

212
00:15:05,839 --> 00:15:11,092
the tag v0.2.0. That's fact c. A tag in
the list means a release contains the

213
00:15:11,132 --> 00:15:11,573
secret.

214
00:15:11,913 --> 00:15:15,512
Three commits lie between the
first one and the server's tip.

215
00:15:15,552 --> 00:15:19,211
And over all commits, the key
string occurs in five snapshots.

216
00:15:19,551 --> 00:15:23,244
The last command is the hint
for the second half of fact b.

217
00:15:23,284 --> 00:15:27,104
The reflog is Git's local record
of the values a ref has had.

218
00:15:27,144 --> 00:15:32,166
The reflog of origin/main in your clone
records when your clone saw the server's

219
00:15:32,206 --> 00:15:37,101
branch change, here "update by push".
It's your clone's view, not the server's

220
00:15:37,141 --> 00:15:40,455
record. For the server's
side you go to the platform.

221
00:15:40,795 --> 00:15:45,980
Now the facts that got no command. Fact
a: which secret and what it can reach.

222
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:51,138
That's a question about the credential's
type and scope, from the last video.

223
00:15:51,178 --> 00:15:56,765
Fact d: who could read it. That's repository
visibility, forks, collaborators and CI

224
00:15:56,805 --> 00:16:01,588
logs: platform state. And fact e: was
it used. Only the provider's logs.

225
00:16:01,628 --> 00:16:03,263
Git answered two of five.

226
00:16:03,603 --> 00:16:08,563
The replay continues with eradication:
removing the file at the tip, a rewrite, a

227
00:16:08,603 --> 00:16:13,625
mirrored force push, pruning. Those are 🔴
DANGEROUS operations, and each needs its

228
00:16:13,665 --> 00:16:18,688
five answers. They are the subject of the
next two videos, together with the stale

229
00:16:18,728 --> 00:16:23,565
clone that pushes the secret back. For
this incident, ask the question from the

230
00:16:23,605 --> 00:16:28,442
concept section first: the key is a
revocable API key, and it has been revoked.

231
00:16:28,482 --> 00:16:32,331
Is a rewrite warranted? Write
down your answer and your reason.

232
00:16:32,371 --> 00:16:35,109
Exercise 31.3 is about
exactly that decision.

233
00:16:36,949 --> 00:16:38,597
Five mistakes to watch for.

234
00:16:38,937 --> 00:16:43,715
Starting with the repository. Root cause:
the damage happens at the issuer, where

235
00:16:43,755 --> 00:16:48,117
the key is accepted; until revocation
the key works for whoever copied it.

236
00:16:48,457 --> 00:16:53,354
Rewriting history by reflex. Root cause:
for a revoked key the rewrite removes

237
00:16:53,394 --> 00:16:58,670
nothing that still matters, and it costs
every collaborator their clone, invalidates

238
00:16:58,710 --> 00:17:01,329
recorded commit IDs and strips signatures.

239
00:17:01,669 --> 00:17:05,116
Rotating one credential after
a runtime was compromised.

240
00:17:05,156 --> 00:17:09,974
Root cause: the scope is every credential
reachable from that runtime; partial

241
00:17:10,014 --> 00:17:12,466
rotation is how a second breach happens.

242
00:17:12,806 --> 00:17:16,165
Reporting "we found no use"
from Git or from GitHub alone.

243
00:17:16,205 --> 00:17:20,853
Root cause: whether the key was used is
recorded at the issuer; Git answers only

244
00:17:20,893 --> 00:17:22,553
which commits and which refs.

245
00:17:22,893 --> 00:17:27,791
Sending the alert to one person. Root
cause: an alert in one inbox is not an

246
00:17:27,831 --> 00:17:32,730
incident process; the xAI key stayed
valid two months after the first alert.

247
00:17:34,570 --> 00:17:37,965
Now, out of the lab. Picture
an AI company on a Tuesday.

248
00:17:38,005 --> 00:17:42,933
A scanner alert arrives for a provider key
in a repository of evaluation scripts.

249
00:17:42,973 --> 00:17:47,779
The engineer on call follows the six
steps, and writes one line per step in the

250
00:17:47,819 --> 00:17:48,822
incident channel.

251
00:17:49,162 --> 00:17:54,007
At nine fourteen, contain: key revoked in
the provider's console, new key issued,

252
00:17:54,047 --> 00:17:56,601
and the two services
that use it are named.

253
00:17:56,941 --> 00:18:01,959
At nine thirty-one, assess, five facts.
The secret: a provider key with access to

254
00:18:01,999 --> 00:18:07,017
the company's fine-tuned models. First
commit and first push: an ID, an author, a

255
00:18:07,057 --> 00:18:11,638
date, and pushed the same day according
to the platform's activity record.

256
00:18:11,678 --> 00:18:16,634
Refs: main and one release tag. Who could
read: the repository is internal, with

257
00:18:16,674 --> 00:18:21,630
forty collaborators, and one CI log printed
the variable name but not the value.

258
00:18:21,670 --> 00:18:26,875
Was it used: the provider's log shows no
calls from unknown addresses in the window.

259
00:18:27,215 --> 00:18:31,885
At nine forty, eradicate: the file
is removed from the tip and ignored.

260
00:18:31,925 --> 00:18:37,390
Rewrite decision: not warranted. The key
is revoked and the provider's logs show no

261
00:18:37,430 --> 00:18:38,783
use. Reason recorded.

262
00:18:39,123 --> 00:18:44,023
At five past ten, recover: both services
run with the new key, and the alert is

263
00:18:44,063 --> 00:18:45,149
closed as revoked.

264
00:18:45,489 --> 00:18:50,083
At ten past ten, communicate: a short note
to the team with what happened and that

265
00:18:50,123 --> 00:18:53,531
no action on clones is needed,
because nothing was rewritten.

266
00:18:53,871 --> 00:18:58,843
Afternoon, prevent: push protection is
enabled for the repository, and the team's

267
00:18:58,883 --> 00:19:03,793
guide gains one sentence about staging
named files and reading git diff --cached

268
00:19:03,833 --> 00:19:05,031
before every commit.

269
00:19:05,371 --> 00:19:10,069
Under an hour from the alert to recovery,
and the first step did most of the work.

270
00:19:11,909 --> 00:19:16,996
Your turn. Do Exercise
31.2, "Fix the order", in

271
00:19:17,036 --> 00:19:21,055
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

272
00:19:21,395 --> 00:19:26,188
Before you rearrange anything, predict for
each step in the exercise's sequence what

273
00:19:26,228 --> 00:19:30,331
is still possible for an attacker
while the team is busy with that step.

274
00:19:30,371 --> 00:19:35,049
The step after which the answer becomes
"nothing" is the one that must come first.

275
00:19:35,389 --> 00:19:40,126
The challenge is Exercise 31.3,
"Rewrite or not?", in the same file.

276
00:19:41,966 --> 00:19:45,181
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

277
00:19:50,021 --> 00:19:54,948
Notice the word "investigate", and don't
let it pull you into the repository first.

278
00:19:54,988 --> 00:19:59,856
A strong answer says what happens before
the investigation and why, then gives the

279
00:19:59,896 --> 00:20:04,583
investigation as the five facts, each
with the place the answer comes from: the

280
00:20:04,623 --> 00:20:07,875
credential's definition, Git,
the platform, the issuer.

281
00:20:07,915 --> 00:20:10,986
It names the commands for
the two facts Git answers.

282
00:20:11,326 --> 00:20:16,018
It ends with the decision the investigation
feeds, and with what is recorded.

283
00:20:16,058 --> 00:20:20,565
The follow-up is the board asking,
months later, whether anyone cloned the

284
00:20:20,605 --> 00:20:25,665
repository with a leaked GitHub token:
recall from video 164 what a search by token

285
00:20:25,705 --> 00:20:30,827
hash returns, how long Git events are
retained, and what had to be set up before the

286
00:20:30,867 --> 00:20:31,380
incident.

287
00:20:33,220 --> 00:20:34,147
Let's land this.

288
00:20:34,487 --> 00:20:36,147
You should now be able to say:

289
00:20:36,487 --> 00:20:41,103
The six steps are contain, assess, eradicate,
recover, communicate, prevent, and the

290
00:20:41,143 --> 00:20:42,988
order matters more than the tools.

291
00:20:43,328 --> 00:20:48,174
Revocation comes first because the damage
happens at the issuer, and it is the only

292
00:20:48,214 --> 00:20:52,001
step that works against every copy;
it may be all that is needed.

293
00:20:52,341 --> 00:20:56,809
An assessment produces five facts: the
secret and its reach, the first commit and

294
00:20:56,849 --> 00:21:01,317
first push, the refs, who could read it,
and whether it was used; Git answers the

295
00:21:01,357 --> 00:21:04,378
second and third, and only
the issuer answers the last.

296
00:21:04,718 --> 00:21:09,680
Rewrite history when the data stays harmful
after rotation or cannot be rotated, not

297
00:21:09,720 --> 00:21:12,598
by reflex for a revoked
key; record the decision.

298
00:21:12,938 --> 00:21:17,773
The cases repeat one root cause: a long-lived,
over-scoped credential written where

299
00:21:17,813 --> 00:21:19,301
it should never have been.

300
00:21:21,141 --> 00:21:26,630
Read sections 21B.14 and
21B.15 of Chapter 21B. Read

301
00:21:26,670 --> 00:21:30,459
cheatsheets/security-cheat-sheet.md.

302
00:21:30,799 --> 00:21:35,805
Today you put a leak response in order,
and saw an incident shrink before anyone

303
00:21:35,845 --> 00:21:40,915
touched the repository. Say the six steps
aloud, in order, before the next video.

304
00:21:40,955 --> 00:21:45,772
Next time: history rewriting as an
operation, and its mechanics seen locally.

305
00:21:45,812 --> 00:21:49,052
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

306
00:21:49,092 --> 00:21:50,566
See you in the next one.
